        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Ibn Battuta</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">1304 CE – c. 1368 CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Born</span><span class="info-stat-val">24 February 1304, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marinid-dynasty">Marinid dynasty</a>; of Arabised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> descent, from the Lawata clan and a family of Islamic legal scholars</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Died</span><span class="info-stat-val">1368 or 1369, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, where he had been appointed a judge; little is known of his last years</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Who they were</span><span class="info-stat-val">Traveller and scholar; qadi (judge) under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bin-tughluq">Muhammad bin Tughluq</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>; chief qadi in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maldives">Maldives</a>; envoy charged with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>'s embassy to Yuan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> (from 1341); judge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> after 1354</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">Travels of around 117,000 km over about thirty years; four pilgrimages to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>; service as a judge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maldives">Maldives</a>; journeys to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> coast and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a>; The Rihla, dictated to Ibn Juzayy; doubts over how much of it he saw himself</span></div>
