    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Ibn Battuta left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a> in 1325 to make the pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> and did not return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> for 24 years, travelling through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</li><li>He lived by his learning in Islamic law, serving as a judge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maldives">Maldives</a>, and grew wealthy on the gifts of rulers, among them the slaves he was given and bought.</li><li>His account of the journeys, dictated to Ibn Juzayy after 1354, is the only source for his life; scholars have shown that parts of it were copied from earlier travellers and doubt that he saw every place he describes.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The patronymic Ibn Battuta literally means 'son of a duckling'; in the Rihla he gives his full name as Shams al-Din Abu 'Abdallah Muhammad ibn 'Abdallah ibn Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Yusuf Lawati al-Tanji ibn Battuta.</p></aside>
    </section>

