    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1304</span><span class="rf-text">Ibn Battuta is born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a>, into a family of Islamic legal scholars</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1325</span><span class="rf-text">He sets out from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a>, aged 21, on the pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1326</span><span class="rf-text">He completes his first pilgrimage at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> and decides to travel on to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1333</span><span class="rf-text">He and his party reach the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a>, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bin-tughluq">Muhammad bin Tughluq</a> appoints him a qadi</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1341</span><span class="rf-text">He is given charge of an embassy to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and leaves <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1345</span><span class="rf-text">He reaches <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quanzhou">Quanzhou</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, then under the Mongol-led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1352</span><span class="rf-text">He crosses the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> with a caravan to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1354</span><span class="rf-text">He returns to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> and dictates the account of his travels to Ibn Juzayy</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>All that is known of Ibn Battuta's life comes from the account of his travels. It records that he was of Arabised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> descent, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a> on 24 February 1304, during the reign of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marinid-dynasty">Marinid dynasty</a>, into a family of Islamic legal scholars, known in the Muslim traditions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadi">qadis</a>. His family belonged to a Berber tribal clan, the Lawata. His most common full name is given as Abu Abdullah Muhammad ibn Battuta, a patronymic meaning 'son of a duckling'.</p><p>As a young man he would have studied at a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki school</a>, the dominant form of education in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> at the time, and that training in law was to be his living abroad: Maliki Muslims elsewhere asked him to serve as their religious judge because he came from a land where their school was practised.</p><p>On 2 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajab">Rajab</a> 725 AH, 14 June 1325, he left his home town at the age of 21 to make the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">hajj</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, a journey that would ordinarily take sixteen months. He was, by his own account, eager to learn about far-away lands. He set out alone, he wrote, "swayed by an overmastering impulse within me", and grieved at leaving his parents, both still living. He would not see Morocco again for 24 years.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>He went overland along the North African coast, through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bejaia">Béjaïa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>, usually joining a caravan for safety, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sfax">Sfax</a> took the first of the many wives of his travels. In the early spring of 1326 he reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahri-mamluks">Bahri Mamluk</a> realm, where, by his account, the ascetic Sheikh Burhanuddin foretold that he would travel to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> a rebellion turned him back from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>, and he went instead through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, completing his first pilgrimage in November 1326 and taking the honorific El-Hajji.</p><p>Rather than return home, he travelled on: through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, where he joined for a while the caravan of Abu Sa'id, the last Mongol ruler of the unified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>, then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>. He stayed in Mecca for a time, perhaps three years, though problems of chronology lead commentators to suggest he left after the 1328 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">hajj</a>. He sailed down the Red Sea to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a>, then along the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a> to Mogadishu and down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> coast to Kilwa, whose ruler he praised. In the autumn of 1330 or 1332 he set out to seek employment with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bin-tughluq">Muhammad bin Tughluq</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, by way of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a>. His party reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> on 12 September 1333.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> the sultan, known as the wealthiest man in the Muslim world, appointed him a qadi on the strength of his years of study in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>. For six years he veered between the high life of a trusted subordinate and suspicion of treason. In 1341 he was given charge of an embassy to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>; on the way bandits robbed and nearly killed him, and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calicut">Calicut</a> a storm sank one of the expedition's ships while the other sailed without him. Afraid to return to Delhi a failure, he stayed in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and then went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maldives">Maldives</a>.</p><p>There he spent nine months as chief judge, married into the royal family of Omar I and took four wives. He tried to bring local practice closer to a strict application of Muslim law, ordering that men who missed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khutba">Friday prayer</a> be publicly whipped and that robbers' right hands be cut off, until his judgements set the rulers against him and he resigned. By way of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai-sultanate">Madurai Sultanate</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chittagong">Chittagong</a>, where in 1345 he met the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> Shah Jalal, he sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samudera-pasai-sultanate">Samudra Pasai</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> and on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quanzhou">Quanzhou</a> in China in 1345. He described its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paper-money">paper money</a>, went on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuzhou">Fuzhou</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangzhou">Hangzhou</a>, and says he reached the Yuan court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-huizong">Emperor Huizong</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Rihla records the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> that ran through his travels. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> the ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">Beylik</a> of Aydin gave him his first slave; he bought a young Greek girl for 40 dinars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> and was given and bought others. He insulted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> as "enemies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a>", drunkards and "swine eaters" while keeping a Greek girl among the slave girls of his "harem". He took wives wherever he stayed and divorced them when he moved on, had children by wives or concubines in several cities, and waited two decades to learn what had become of a wife and child in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> he wrote that black Africans were characterised by "ill manners" and that he "was long astonished at their feeble intellect"; he left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> with a caravan carrying 600 female slaves.</p><p>The account itself has long been questioned. He seems to have kept no notes, and Ibn Juzayy copied passages from Ibn Jubayr, writing more than 150 years earlier, and from Muhammad al-Abdari without saying so. Modern scholars doubt that he visited every place described: a trip up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolghar">Bolghar</a> is thought very unlikely, and his journeys to Sanaa and around Anatolia are in doubt. Some question whether he reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, where his account shares material with Shihab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-umari">al-Umari</a> and others and confuses the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a>. Henry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yule">Yule</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-henry-scott">William Henry Scott</a> thought the princess Urduja and her land of Tawalisi entirely fictitious, and the German scholar Ralph Elger suspects much of the work to be a fiction compiled from other travel reports.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>He left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quanzhou">Quanzhou</a> in 1346 and began the journey home. In the Strait of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormuz">Hormuz</a> he learned that Abu Sa'id had died and his lands had collapsed into civil war. In 1348 he reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, where he learned that his father had died fifteen years before, and he travelled on through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a>: the toll in Damascus, he heard, reached 2,400 a day, Gaza was depopulated and deaths in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> were reported at 1,100 a day. After a last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">hajj</a> he came back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a> by way of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> in 1349, to find that his mother too had died a few months earlier.</p><p>In 1350 he joined Muslims going to defend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-xi-of-castile">Alfonso XI of Castile</a>, but the plague had killed the king and he toured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> instead. In February 1352 he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sijilmasa">Sijilmasa</a> with a caravan across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>, by the salt mines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taghaza">Taghaza</a> and Oualata, to the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a>, where he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa">Mansa</a> Suleyman, and went on by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> to Gao. In the desert a message from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> commanded him home, and he returned early in 1354.</p><p>At the suggestion of the Marinid ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-inan-faris">Abu Inan Faris</a> he dictated an account of his journeys to the scholar Ibn Juzayy, completed in 1355. Little is known of his life after that. He was appointed a judge in Morocco and died in 1368 or 1369.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Rihla, whose full title may be translated as A Masterpiece to Those Who Contemplate the Wonders of Cities and the Marvels of Travelling, was unknown outside the Muslim world until the early 19th century. Ulrich Jasper Seetzen acquired an abridged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>, Johann Kosegarten published extracts in 1818, and Samuel Lee's English translation of another abridgement appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1829. In the 1830s, during the French occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, the Bibliothèque Nationale acquired five manuscripts, one dated 1356 and believed to be Ibn Juzayy's autograph, from which Charles Defrémery and Beniamino Sanguinetti published from 1853 the Arabic text with a French translation. Hamilton Gibb's English translation for the Hakluyt Society was completed by Charles Beckingham in 1994.</p><p>Even if it is not fully based on what its author witnessed, the Rihla provides an important account of much of the 14th-century world, and his itinerary shows scholars when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> first began to spread into the heart of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>. It holds the earliest mention of the Mande xylophone. By its reckoning he travelled around 117,000 km, more than any other explorer in pre-modern history. In 1987 Ross E. Dunn doubted that evidence would be found to support the narrative, but in 2010 Tim Mackintosh-Smith reported manuscripts of Islamic law copied by Ibn Battuta in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> in 1326, which bear out the Rihla's date. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a>'s airport and a mall in Dubai bear his name.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">On the globe, stop by stop</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/journeys?j=ibn-battuta">The Travels of Ibn Battuta<span>Journey</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Ibn Battuta, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a> in 1304, left on the pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> in 1325 and travelled for nearly thirty years across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, Asia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>, serving as a judge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maldives">Maldives</a>. The Rihla he dictated after 1354 is the only source for his life, and scholars doubt that he saw all he describes.</em></p>

